 private static readonly Entry[] Entries=[
 new(111,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L09","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-9/preparation.html","4fb3d4ecbac1648e7aa09b95fd8a7c7395e8ad06b903b0b8c08681748cc050ec",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.9"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will add 2 three-digit numbers.\nIn previous lessons, you added a two-digit number and a three-digit number. You shared and compared methods and representations that showed adding by place and composing units. In this lesson, you practice adding within 1,000.","Key ideas:\n- Add 2 three-digit numbers using place value strategies that include composing 2 units\n- In previous lessons, you added a two-digit number and a three-digit number\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(528 + 2\\) \\(528 + 7\\) \\(487 + 3\\) \\(487 + 8\\)\nApproach: “What do you notice about the sums?” (I made a ten to find the sum. You all show adding some ones to three-digit numbers. There’s a pattern in the ones—0 ones, 5 ones, 0 ones, 5 ones.) “Why are \\(528 + 2\\) and \\(487 + 3\\) helpful in finding the value of the other expressions?” (We know you make a new ten, so it helped me think about how I could decompose a number to help make a ten.)\nExample 2: Find the value of each expression. Show your thinking. \\(384 + 409\\) \\(757 + 152\\) \\(262 + 438\\) \\(575 + 166\\)\nApproach: Represent the quantities, write a matching addition relationship, then combine or count on to find and check the total.","Step 1: Identify the quantities being combined.\nStep 2: Represent each quantity with objects, a drawing, number line, or expression.\nStep 3: Write an addition relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the total using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check that the total matches the original quantities.","Common mistake: counting an item twice or combining the wrong quantities. Check the addends and verify the total.","You will add 2 three-digit numbers. In previous lessons, you added a two-digit number and a three-digit number."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit7 Lesson9 Add Three-digit Numbers. Source-specific authentic mathematical evidence: Warm-up 528+2, 528+7, 487+3, 487+8. Activity1 independent and peer method comparisons: 384+409=793, 757+152=909, 262+438=700, 575+166=741. Activity2 Noah and Kiran each show 267+338=605; Noah’s pictured base-ten representation has 5 hundreds, 9 tens, 15 ones but fails to add the composed ten to tens and then form a hundred. Goal: Add TWO three-digit numbers by place, correctly compose units and critique the mistaken represented student method. Original media and open-task boundary: Real Noah/Kiran diagram images and genuine students’ independent diagrams, discussions and multiple strategies demand teacher; no fabricated screenshot or protected Cool-down.","Exact mathematics and representations: Add TWO three-digit numbers by place, correctly compose units and critique the mistaken represented student method. CCSS source references: CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.9. Edulytics FIRST-PARTY example families are independent, not the original publisher question set.","Public student-facing authentic numbers and instructions: Warm-up 528+2, 528+7, 487+3, 487+8. Activity1 independent and peer method comparisons: 384+409=793, 757+152=909, 262+438=700, 575+166=741. Activity2 Noah and Kiran each show 267+338=605; Noah’s pictured base-ten representation has 5 hundreds, 9 tens, 15 ones but fails to add the composed ten to tens and then form a hundred. First-party companion questions generate fresh values, preserve the identified skill and separate paper/partner work from numeric self-check. Real Noah/Kiran diagram images and genuine students’ independent diagrams, discussions and multiple strategies demand teacher; no fabricated screenshot or protected Cool-down.","Step1 identify source task and given quantities. Step2 represent with base-ten blocks, precise number line or appropriate chart; keep hidden publisher data unknown. Step3 identify units and whether compose/decompose none, a ten, a hundred, or both. Step4 calculate independent FIRST-PARTY numbers with explicit place-value regrouping or justified counting on. Step5 verify sum/subtraction using reverse operation and check number bounds. Step6 original card-sort/graph construction/partner game or flexible student explanation needs educator and never gets fabricated automatic certification. Source objective: Add TWO three-digit numbers by place, correctly compose units and critique the mistaken represented student method.","Avoid treating every operation as generic 3-digit arithmetic, neglecting a composed or decomposed ten/hundred, treating a personally selected method as uniquely correct or guessing unseen card/bar/image values. Source-specific safeguard: Real Noah/Kiran diagram images and genuine students’ independent diagrams, discussions and multiple strategies demand teacher; no fabricated screenshot or protected Cool-down.","G2 U07 L9 Add TWO three-digit numbers by place, correctly compose units and critique the mistaken represented student method. Original evidence: Warm-up 528+2, 528+7, 487+3, 487+8. Activity1 independent and peer method comparisons: 384+409=793, 757+152=909, 262+438=700, 575+166=741. Activity2 Noah and Kiran each show 267+338=605; Noah’s pictured base-ten representation has 5 hundreds, 9 tens, 15 ones but fails to add the composed ten to tens and then form a hundred. Human limits: Real Noah/Kiran diagram images and genuine students’ independent diagrams, discussions and multiple strategies demand teacher; no fabricated screenshot or protected Cool-down."}),
 new(112,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L10","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-10/preparation.html","c672c392ff6458929f0a5d04442397336cd694efb2e5adda5cd8d80e87a3cdc1",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.9"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will add three-digit numbers using strategies based on place value and the properties of operations.\nIn previous lessons, you used properties of operations, your understanding of the count sequence, and your understanding of place value to add within 1,000. You used different methods and representations to add by place and compose tens and hundreds. In this lesson, you choose your own methods for adding within 1,000 and share your methods with your peers.","Key ideas:\n- Add numbers within 1,000 using strategies based on place value and the properties of operations\n- In previous lessons, you used properties of operations, your understanding of the count sequence, and your understanding of place value to add within 1,000\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(199 + 23\\) \\(198 + 24\\) \\(297 + 25\\) \\(395 + 27\\)\nApproach: “When finding \\(199 + 23\\) , we could think about adding by place and composing a ten and a hundred.” “Some people thought about counting on to get to the next hundred and then added what was left. Why is that a useful method?” (199 is really close to a new hundred. It’s easier for me to add \\(200 + 22\\) in my head than try to add by place in my head.)\nExample 2: Sort the cards into 2 groups with your partner. Make a group of expressions that you agree the value is less challenging to find. Make another group of expressions that you agree the value is more challenging to find. Keep any expressions together that you and your partner disagree on. Choose an expression that you feel is less challenging. Find the...\nApproach: Represent the quantities, write a matching addition relationship, then combine or count on to find and check the total.","Step 1: Identify the quantities being combined.\nStep 2: Represent each quantity with objects, a drawing, number line, or expression.\nStep 3: Write an addition relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the total using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check that the total matches the original quantities.","Common mistake: counting an item twice or combining the wrong quantities. Check the addends and verify the total.","You will add three-digit numbers using strategies based on place value and the properties of operations. In previous lessons, you used properties of operations, your understanding of the count sequence, and your understanding of place value to add within 1,000."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit7 Lesson10 Add within 1,000. Source-specific authentic mathematical evidence: Warm-up mentally 199+23=222, 198+24=222, 297+25=322,395+27=422. Activity1 sort actual How Did You Do That? cards into more/less challenging additions and defend relative difficulty and selected strategy; source card art not copied. Activity2 Diego masked addends 900+?=1000→100, ?+430=1000→570, ?85+615=1000→385. Final compare 429+387=816 versus 498+387=885. Goal: Choose addition method for mental complements/three-digit sums and recover masked three-digit unknowns to 1,000. Original media and open-task boundary: Actual original printed source cards and paint-covered digit images and personal easier/harder judgments require human review. Exact independently-authored numeric companions do not grade these.","Exact mathematics and representations: Choose addition method for mental complements/three-digit sums and recover masked three-digit unknowns to 1,000. CCSS source references: CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.9. Edulytics FIRST-PARTY example families are independent, not the original publisher question set.","Public student-facing authentic numbers and inst