l evidence: Warm-up mentally 199+23=222, 198+24=222, 297+25=322,395+27=422. Activity1 sort actual How Did You Do That? cards into more/less challenging additions and defend relative difficulty and selected strategy; source card art not copied. Activity2 Diego masked addends 900+?=1000→100, ?+430=1000→570, ?85+615=1000→385. Final compare 429+387=816 versus 498+387=885. Human limits: Actual original printed source cards and paint-covered digit images and personal easier/harder judgments require human review. Exact independently-authored numeric companions do not grade these."}),
 new(113,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L11","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-11/preparation.html","3df2be663d16bcfd878e27b3febe9310b505e6435ab8c98a98cc3eaff6318254",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.6","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will practice adding numbers within 1,000.\nThis lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need. In Activity 1, you learn stage 4 of the How Close center. In this new stage, called Add to 1,000, you pick 8 digit cards and then choose 6 of those to make an expression that yields a number as close as possible to the target number, 1,000.","Key ideas:\n- Add numbers within 1,000\n- This lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(80¢ + 20¢ + 37¢\\) \\(80¢ + 20¢+ 37¢+ 42¢\\) \\(75¢ + 37¢ + 25¢\\) \\(75¢ + 80¢ + 25¢ + 20¢\\)\nApproach: Represent the quantities or relationships clearly, apply the lesson idea, and check that the result answers the question.","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will practice adding numbers within 1,000. This lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit7 Lesson11 Center Day 2 (optional). Source-specific authentic mathematical evidence: OPTIONAL warm-up cash number talk: 80¢+20¢+37¢=137¢, then +42¢=179¢;75¢+37¢+25¢=137¢;75¢+80¢+25¢+20¢=200¢. Activity1 How Close? Stage4 remove 10-cards, pick 8 digit cards, choose 5 or 6 to make two three-digit addends, minimize difference from 1,000 across 10 rounds. Activity2 Five in a Row Stage8 add within 1,000 WITH composing units, real counters, two paper clips and partner choices. Goal: Practice more than two addends and composing via real center games, not a fixed invented publisher card set. Original media and open-task boundary: No predetermined original source card digits, scores, actual game board/wins or partner choice; all need educator, cannot auto certify.","Exact mathematics and representations: Practice more than two addends and composing via real center games, not a fixed invented publisher card set. CCSS source references: CCSS:2.NBT.B.6, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7. Edulytics FIRST-PARTY example families are independent, not the original publisher question set.","Public student-facing authentic numbers and instructions: OPTIONAL warm-up cash number talk: 80¢+20¢+37¢=137¢, then +42¢=179¢;75¢+37¢+25¢=137¢;75¢+80¢+25¢+20¢=200¢. Activity1 How Close? Stage4 remove 10-cards, pick 8 digit cards, choose 5 or 6 to make two three-digit addends, minimize difference from 1,000 across 10 rounds. Activity2 Five in a Row Stage8 add within 1,000 WITH composing units, real counters, two paper clips and partner choices. First-party companion questions generate fresh values, preserve the identified skill and separate paper/partner work from numeric self-check. No predetermined original source card digits, scores, actual game board/wins or partner choice; all need educator, cannot auto certify.","Step1 identify source task and given quantities. Step2 represent with base-ten blocks, precise number line or appropriate chart; keep hidden publisher data unknown. Step3 identify units and whether compose/decompose none, a ten, a hundred, or both. Step4 calculate independent FIRST-PARTY numbers with explicit place-value regrouping or justified counting on. Step5 verify sum/subtraction using reverse operation and check number bounds. Step6 original card-sort/graph construction/partner game or flexible student explanation needs educator and never gets fabricated automatic certification. Source objective: Practice more than two addends and composing via real center games, not a fixed invented publisher card set.","Avoid treating every operation as generic 3-digit arithmetic, neglecting a composed or decomposed ten/hundred, treating a personally selected method as uniquely correct or guessing unseen card/bar/image values. Source-specific safeguard: No predetermined original source card digits, scores, actual game board/wins or partner choice; all need educator, cannot auto certify.","G2 U07 L11 Practice more than two addends and composing via real center games, not a fixed invented publisher card set. Original evidence: OPTIONAL warm-up cash number talk: 80¢+20¢+37¢=137¢, then +42¢=179¢;75¢+37¢+25¢=137¢;75¢+80¢+25¢+20¢=200¢. Activity1 How Close? Stage4 remove 10-cards, pick 8 digit cards, choose 5 or 6 to make two three-digit addends, minimize difference from 1,000 across 10 rounds. Activity2 Five in a Row Stage8 add within 1,000 WITH composing units, real counters, two paper clips and partner choices. Human limits: No predetermined original source card digits, scores, actual game board/wins or partner choice; all need educator, cannot auto certify."}),
 new(114,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L12","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-12/preparation.html","e92d5358ee0ff2900186e485200b249fb36135a16fea4b107a9d5026044724b6",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.1","CCSS:2.NBT.A.3","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will decompose a ten in order to subtract within 1,000.\nIn previous units, you used methods based on place value to decompose a ten when subtracting within 100. In this lesson, you extend your understanding of decomposing tens to subtract by place within 1,000. In the first activity, you are invited to use whatever method makes sense to them to subtract one- and two-digit numbers from a three-digit number.","Key ideas:\n- Subtract numbers within 1,000 using place value strategies that include decomposing a ten\n- In previous units, you used methods based on place value to decompose a ten when subtracting within 100\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression in any way that makes sense to you. Explain or show your reasoning. \\(354 - 7\\) \\(354 - 36\\) \\(354 - 48\\)\nApproach: “What is the same and what is different about the ways ___ and ___ represented the problem?” ( ___ used base-ten blocks and showed decomposing a ten. ___ showed a number line and counting back 36. You used the same numbers. You found the same difference.)\nExample 2: Work with your partner to find the value of each expression. Partner A: Read the expression and represent the larger number using blocks. Partner B: Decide if you will decompose a ten and explain. Then subtract. Discuss and write the difference. Switch roles. \\(264 - 38\\) \\(274 - 41\\) \\(336 - 115\\) \\(343 - 127\\) \\(485 - 266\\) \\(451 - 315\\)\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.","Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.","Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.","You will decompose a ten in order to subtract within 1,000. In previous units, you used methods based on place value to decompose a ten when subtracting within 100."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit7 Lesson12 Decompose to Subtract. Source-specific authentic mathematical evidence: Warm-up describe 354 as 3 hundreds 5 tens 4 ones or renaming equivalent units. Activity1 subtract 354−7=347,354−36=318,354−48=306, each requires decomposing a ten by place. Activity2 source base-ten partner list: 264−38=226,274−41=233,336−115=221,343−127=216,485−266=219,451−315=136. Goal: Decompose a ten (10 ones) to subtract beyond 100 and compare blocks, open number line, equations or student written proof. Original media and open-task boundary: Actual physical blocks, student shown borrow/renaming, explanations and publisher Cool-down are teacher-reviewed.","Exact mathematics and representations: Decompose a ten (10 ones) to subtract beyond 100 and compare blocks, open number line, equations or student written proof. CCSS source references: CCSS:2.NBT.A.1, CCSS:2.NBT.A.3, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7. Edulytics FIRST-PARTY example families are independent, not the original publisher question set.","Public student-facing authentic numbers and instructions: Warm-up describe 354 as 3 hundreds 5 tens 4 ones or renaming equivalent units. Activity1 subtract 354−7=347,354−36=318,354−48=306, each requires decomposing a ten by place. Activity2 source base-ten partner list: 264−38=226,274−41=233,336−115=221,343−127=216,485−266=219,451−315=136. First-party companion questions generate fresh values, preserve the identified skill and separate paper/partner work from numeric self-check. Actual physical blocks, student shown borrow/renaming, explanations and publisher Cool-down are teacher-reviewed.","Step1 identify source task and given quantities. Step2 represent with base-ten blocks, precise number line or appropriate chart; keep hidden publisher data unknown. Step3 identify units and whether compose/decompose none, a ten, a hundred, or both. Step4 calculate independent FIRST-PARTY numbers with explicit place-value regrouping or justified counting on. Step5 verify sum/subtraction using reverse operation and check number bounds. Step6 original card-sort/graph construction/partner game or flexible student explanation needs educator and never gets fabricated automatic certification. Source objective: Decompose a ten (10 ones) to subtract beyond 100 and compare blocks, open number line, equations or student written proof.","Avoid treating every operation as generic 3-digit arithmetic, neglecting a composed or decomposed ten/hundred, treating a personally selected method as uniquely correct or guessing unseen card/bar/image values. Source-specific safeguard: Actual physical blocks, student shown borrow/renaming, explanations and publisher Cool-down are teacher-reviewed.","G2 U07 L12 Decompose a ten (10 ones) to subtract beyond 100 and compare blocks, open number line, equations or student written proof. Original evidence: Warm-up describe 354 as 3 hundreds 5 tens 4 ones or renaming equivalent units. Activity1 subtract 354−7=347,354−36=318,354−48=306, each requires decomposing a ten by place. Activity2 source base-ten partner list: 264−38=226,274−41=233,336−115=221,343−127=216,485−266=219,451−315=136. Human limits: Actual physical blocks, student shown borrow/renaming, explanations and publisher Cool-down are teacher-reviewed."}),
 new(115,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L13","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-13/preparation.html","30064d2dd3c40c98debe3c4672601e511cdbf321a49992a64cee48ef2506c0ca",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.9"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will subtract from three-digit numbers using place value strategies that include decomposing a ten or hundred.\nIn a previous lesson, you subtracted from a three-digit number using place value strategies that included decomposing a ten. In this lesson, you interpret representations of subtraction that show decomposing a ten and hundred. You use base-ten blocks and your understanding of place value to explain why the method works (MP7).","Key ideas:\n- Subtract numbers within 1,000 using place value strategies that include decomposing a ten or hundred\n- In a previous lesson, you subtracted from a three-digit number using place value strategies that included decomposing a ten\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.","Example 1: Which one doesn’t belong? A Caption: A B Caption: B C Caption: C D Caption: D\nApproach: “Which images could show a way to decompose a hundred? Explain.” (B because 10 tens is the same as a hundred. A is close, but I think it shows composing a hundred.) “Which images do not show a way to decompose a hundred?” (C because 10 ones are not the same as a hundred. D because it could show a ten as 10 ones.)\nExample 2: Mai used base-ten blocks to find the value of \\(336-52\\) . Then, she started making a diagram to show her work. Explain what Mai did in Step 2. Show what Mai should do next to find the difference. Step 1 Step 2 Write each expression next to the matching diagram. Then find the value of each difference. \\(244 - 28\\) \\(256 - 64\\) \\(244 - 64\\)\nApproach: “What did you have to pay attention to as you matched each diagram to an expression?” (I had to look at the numbers that were being subtracted. I looked for where there were more tens or more ones drawn when there weren’t enough tens or ones.)","Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.","Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.","You will subtract from three-digit numbers using place value strategies that include decomposing a ten or hundred. In a previous lesson, you subtracted from a three-digit number using place value strategies that included decomposing a ten."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit7 Lesson13 Decompose Tens or Hundreds. Source-specific authentic mathematical evidence: Warm-up Which One Doesn’t Belong actual block diagrams A ten tens→hundred, B hundred→10 tens, C incorrectly hundred→10 ones, D ten→10 ones. Activity1 interpret Mai 336−52=284, match diagrams to 244−28=216,256−64=192,244−64=180. Activity2 subtract 245−28=217,352−71=281,364−182=182,293−147=146, explaining regroup a ten or hundred and using blocks. Goal: Distinguish decomposing a ten from a hundred and interpret true source diagrams without inventing image identities beyond text. Original media and open-task boundary: Actual source diagrams and genuine student-selected interpretations, videos or blocks require teacher review; automated numeric result alone insufficient.","Exact mathematics and representations: Distinguish decomposing a ten from a hundred and interpret true source diagrams without inventing image identities beyond text. CCSS source references: CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.9. Edulytics FIRST-PARTY example families are independent, not the original publisher question set.",