Warm-up Which One Doesn’t Belong actual block diagrams A ten tens→hundred, B hundred→10 tens, C incorrectly hundred→10 ones, D ten→10 ones. Activity1 interpret Mai 336−52=284, match diagrams to 244−28=216,256−64=192,244−64=180. Activity2 subtract 245−28=217,352−71=281,364−182=182,293−147=146, explaining regroup a ten or hundred and using blocks. Human limits: Actual source diagrams and genuine student-selected interpretations, videos or blocks require teacher review; automated numeric result alone insufficient."}),
 new(116,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L14","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-14/preparation.html","39d38e4d26d5fe0529d824668936c0eabc8cb1985438774d99623507648c79c9",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.1","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.9"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will analyze expressions to determine if a unit will be decomposed before subtracting.\nIn a previous lesson, you subtracted from a three-digit number and decomposed a ten or a hundred to subtract by place. You represented your thinking with base-ten blocks, drawings, words, or numbers and explained the steps of your method. In this lesson, you subtract two-digit numbers from three-digit numbers when 2 units are decomposed.","Key ideas:\n- Subtract a two-digit number from a three-digit number using place value strategies that include decomposing 2 units\n- In a previous lesson, you subtracted from a three-digit number and decomposed a ten or a hundred to subtract by place\n- Measurement units must be equal in size and placed or interpreted without gaps, overlaps, or a shifted starting point.","Example 1: Which one doesn’t belong? A Caption: A B Caption: B C Caption: C D Caption: D\nApproach: “What do A, B, and C have in common?” (You all show 125.) “Why would you want to represent 125 with 12 tens instead of 1 hundred and 2 tens?” (If you are going to subtract more than 2 tens.)\nExample 2: Tyler and Clare are subtracting by place to find the value of \\(244-67\\) . Tyler says he will decompose before he starts. Clare says she agrees. The diagrams show each student’s first step. Tyler: Clare: What is the same about Tyler and Clare’s diagrams? What is different? Work together to complete Tyler’s way and Clare’s way to find the value of...\nApproach: “How were Tyler’s way and Clare’s way the same? How were your methods different?” (You both show decomposing a ten and a hundred to subtract. You both show the same difference. Tyler decomposed a hundred first, then a ten. Clare decomposed a ten first, then a hundred.)","Step 1: Identify what needs to be measured.\nStep 2: Choose the required unit and correct starting point.\nStep 3: Place or interpret equal-size units accurately.\nStep 4: Count or calculate the units and record the measure.\nStep 5: Check the endpoint, scale, and unit label.","Common mistake: using unequal units, leaving gaps, overlapping units, or starting at the wrong point. Check the unit and endpoints.","You will analyze expressions to determine if a unit will be decomposed before subtracting. In a previous lesson, you subtracted from a three-digit number and decomposed a ten or a hundred to subtract by place."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit7 Lesson14 Think Before You Subtract PLC Activity. Source-specific authentic mathematical evidence: Warm-up base-ten representations A/B/C=125 but D=120. Activity1 compare Tyler and Clare 244−67=177, one decomposes hundred first and other ten first; both must preserve 244 before subtraction. Activity2 sort actual 15 expressions by 0,1,2 decompositions: 599−66,449−88,346−78,633−55,237−29,321−34,457−45,735−72,645−87,905−42,693−63,866−58,514−26,387−44,277−65. Choose one solved example from each group and justify. Goal: Predict required number of regrouped units (0,1,2) before subtraction and preserve equivalence in different base-ten renamings. Original media and open-task boundary: Authentic publisher warm-up diagrams, student-sort t-chart and multiple legitimate strategies require teacher. Do not substitute one numeric key for open sorting.","Exact mathematics and representations: Predict required number of regrouped units (0,1,2) before subtraction and preserve equivalence in different base-ten renamings. CCSS source references: CCSS:2.NBT.A.1, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.9. Edulytics FIRST-PARTY example families are independent, not the original publisher question set.","Public student-facing authentic numbers and instructions: Warm-up base-ten representations A/B/C=125 but D=120. Activity1 compare Tyler and Clare 244−67=177, one decomposes hundred first and other ten first; both must preserve 244 before subtraction. Activity2 sort actual 15 expressions by 0,1,2 decompositions: 599−66,449−88,346−78,633−55,237−29,321−34,457−45,735−72,645−87,905−42,693−63,866−58,514−26,387−44,277−65. Choose one solved example from each group and justify. First-party companion questions generate fresh values, preserve the identified skill and separate paper/partner work from numeric self-check. Authentic publisher warm-up diagrams, student-sort t-chart and multiple legitimate strategies require teacher. Do not substitute one numeric key for open sorting.","Step1 identify source task and given quantities. Step2 represent with base-ten blocks, precise number line or appropriate chart; keep hidden publisher data unknown. Step3 identify units and whether compose/decompose none, a ten, a hundred, or both. Step4 calculate independent FIRST-PARTY numbers with explicit place-value regrouping or justified counting on. Step5 verify sum/subtraction using reverse operation and check number bounds. Step6 original card-sort/graph construction/partner game or flexible student explanation needs educator and never gets fabricated automatic certification. Source objective: Predict required number of regrouped units (0,1,2) before subtraction and preserve equivalence in different base-ten renamings.","Avoid treating every operation as generic 3-digit arithmetic, neglecting a composed or decomposed ten/hundred, treating a personally selected method as uniquely correct or guessing unseen card/bar/image values. Source-specific safeguard: Authentic publisher warm-up diagrams, student-sort t-chart and multiple legitimate strategies require teacher. Do not substitute one numeric key for open sorting.","G2 U07 L14 Predict required number of regrouped units (0,1,2) before subtraction and preserve equivalence in different base-ten renamings. Original evidence: Warm-up base-ten representations A/B/C=125 but D=120. Activity1 compare Tyler and Clare 244−67=177, one decomposes hundred first and other ten first; both must preserve 244 before subtraction. Activity2 sort actual 15 expressions by 0,1,2 decompositions: 599−66,449−88,346−78,633−55,237−29,321−34,457−45,735−72,645−87,905−42,693−63,866−58,514−26,387−44,277−65. Choose one solved example from each group and justify. Human limits: Authentic publisher warm-up diagrams, student-sort t-chart and multiple legitimate strategies require teacher. Do not substitute one numeric key for open sorting."}),
 new(117,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L15","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-15/preparation.html","78c45196c086fcaec7aee0981fd6c91394c44f882a1c3584abf551cc1f0a5ea5",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.2","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.9"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will subtract 2 three-digit numbers using strategies based on place value.\nIn previous lessons, you subtracted 2 three-digit numbers and decomposed a ten or a hundred in order to subtract by place. You analyzed problems to anticipate when one or more units may be decomposed and used your analysis to choose your method and your steps. In this lesson, you subtract 2 three-digit numbers that require decomposing a hundred and a ten when subtracting by place.","Key ideas:\n- Subtract 2 three-digit numbers using place value strategies that include decomposing 2 units\n- In previous lessons, you subtracted 2 three-digit numbers and decomposed a ten or a hundred in order to subtract by place\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.","Example 1: Elena’s thinking: Step 1: Step 2: Step 3: Use base-ten blocks or a base-ten diagram to show Elena’s steps. Finish Elena’s work to find the value of \\(726-558\\) . What is another way you could use numbers or equations to show subtracting by place to find the value of \\(726-558\\) ?\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.\nExample 2: Find someone with a different number than you. Find the difference between your numbers. Show your thinking. Trade cards and find a new partner. Partner 1: Partner 2: Partner 3:\nApproach: Represent the whole and the part, write a matching subtraction relationship, then find and check the difference.","Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.","Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.","You will subtract 2 three-digit numbers using strategies based on place value. In previous lessons, you subtracted 2 three-digit numbers and decomposed a ten or a hundred in order to subtract by place."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit7 Lesson15 Decompose a Ten and a Hundred to Subtract. Source-specific authentic mathematical evidence: Count backward by 10 from 590 to 390 (and reason after 390). Activity1 interpret Elena’s base-ten equations 726−558=168 (decompose ten and hundred, 6 hundreds 11 tens 16 ones), and describe alternate valid recordings. Activity2 Walk About and Subtract: original shuffled three-digit cards selected by partners; source example 733−198=535 by counting on 198→200→733. Goal: Decompose a ten and a hundred for three-digit subtraction, recognize equivalent representations and choose efficient counting-on strategy. Original media and open-task boundary: Source publisher card deck, actual walked-around partners, students’ chosen numbers and diagrams cannot be inferred; human check is required.","Exact mathematics and representations: Decompose a ten and a hundred for three-digit subtraction, recognize equivalent representations and choose efficient counting-on strategy. CCSS source references: CCSS:2.NBT.A.2, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.9. Edulytics FIRST-PARTY example families are independent, not the original publisher question set.","Public student-facing authentic numbers and instructions: Count backward by 10 from 590 to 390 (and reason after 390). Activity1 interpret Elena’s base-ten equations 726−558=168 (decompose ten and hundred, 6 hundreds 11 tens 16 ones), and describe alternate valid recordings. Activity2 Walk About and Subtract: original shuffled three-digit cards selected by partners; source example 733−198=535 by counting on 198→200→733. First-party companion questions generate fresh values, preserve the identified skill and separate paper/partner work from numeric self-check. Source publisher card deck, actual walked-around partners, students’ chosen numbers and diagrams cannot be inferred; human check is required.","Step1 identify source task and given quantities. Step2 represent with base-ten blocks, precise number line or appropriate chart; keep hidden publisher data unknown. Step3 identify units and whether compose/decompose none, a ten, a hundred, or both. Step4 calculate independent FIRST-PARTY numbers with explicit place-value regrouping or justified counting on. Step5 verify sum/subtraction using reverse operation and check number bounds. Step6 original card-sort/graph construction/partner game or flexible student explanation needs educator and never gets fabricated automatic certification. Source objective: Decompose a ten and a hundred for three-digit subtraction, recognize equivalent representations and choose efficient counting-on strategy.","Avoid treating every operation as generic 3-digit arithmetic, neglecting a composed or decomposed ten/hundred, treating a personally selected method as uniquely correct or guessing unseen card/bar/image values. Source-specific safeguard: Source publisher card deck, actual walked-around partners, students’ chosen numbers and diagrams cannot be inferred; human check is required.","G2 U07 L15 Decompose a ten and a hundred for three-digit subtraction, recognize equivalent representations and choose efficient counting-on strategy. Original evidence: Count backward by 10 from 590 to 390 (and reason after 390). Activity1 interpret Elena’s base-ten equations 726−558=168 (decompose ten and hundred, 6 hundreds 11 tens 16 ones), and describe alternate valid recordings. Activity2 Walk About and Subtract: original shuffled three-digit cards selected by partners; source example 733−198=535 by counting on 198→200→733. Human limits: Source publisher card deck, actual walked-around partners, students’ chosen numbers and diagrams cannot be inferred; human check is required."}),
 new(118,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L16","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-16/preparation.html","7bb457d7e5f20b336db5403c56a916b837f016b1b602f6884c0f2720ca1c5e1f",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.A.1","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.9"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will choose methods for finding the value of differences based on the numbers being subtracted.\nIn previous lessons, you found subtracted using various methods and representations with an emphasis on strategies based on place value. You analyzed numbers to anticipate whether you may need to decompose one or more units to subtract. In this lesson, you attend to the relationship between numbers in expressions to flexibly subtract.","Key ideas:\n- Subtract numbers within 1,000 using strategies based on place value and the properties of operations\n- In previous lessons, you found subtracted using various methods and representations with an emphasis on strategies based on place value\n- Subtraction can represent taking away, comparing quantities, or finding a missing addend; the representation must match the situation.","Example 1: Decide if each statement is true or false. Be prepared to explain your reasoning. 2 hundreds \\(+\\) 3 tens \\(+\\) 4 ones \\(=\\) 2 hundreds \\(+\\) 3 tens \\(+\\) 14 ones 2 hundreds \\(+\\) 3 tens \\(+\\) 4 ones \\(=\\) 1 hundred \\(+\\) 13 tens \\(+\\) 4 ones 1 hundred \\(+\\) 13 tens \\(+\\) 4 ones \\(=\\) 1 hundred \\(+\\) 12 tens \\(+\\) 14 ones\nApproach: “How could we change the first statement to make it true?” (You could change 3 tens to 2 tens on the right side. You change the left side so it has 14 ones too. You could change the left side so it has 4 tens.)\nExample 2: Lin’s diagram: Jada’s equations: Discuss how Jada’s equations match Lin’s diagram. Finish Jada’s work to find the value of \\(582 - 145.\\) Jada is thinking about how to find the value of \\(402 - 298.\\) Jada says she knows a way to count on to find the difference. She showed her thinking using a number line. Explain Jada’s thinking. Jada says you can’t...\nApproach: “How does Jada’s method for finding the value of \\(402-298\\) work?” Record student explanation using a series of equations. ( \\(298+2= 300\\) , \\(300+100 = 400\\) , \\(400+2 = 402\\) , \\(2+100+2 = 104\\) ) “Why do you think Jada used this strategy?” (She thought you couldn’t decompose. She noticed 298 is close to 300 and 402 is close to 400.) “Do you agree with Jada that you can't decompose 402?” (I agree that there...","Step 1: Identify the whole and the part being removed or compared.\nStep 2: Represent the situation with objects, a drawing, number line, or equation.\nStep 3: Write a subtraction relationship that matches the representation.\nStep 4: Find the difference using an efficient strategy.\nStep 5: Check the result with the original quantities or a related addition fact.","Common mistake: mixing up the whole and the part being removed or compared. Check that the subtraction relationship matches the representation.","You will choose methods for finding the value of differences based on the numbers being subtracted. In previous lessons, you found subtracted using various methods and representations with an emphasis on strategies based on place value."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit7 Lesson16 Subtract Within 1,000. Source-specific authentic mathematical evidence: Warm-up equivalence of regrouped place-value statements; first source assertion 2 hundreds+3 tens+4 ones ≠2 hundreds+3 tens+14 ones, second =1 hundred+13 tens+4 ones, third 1 hundred+13 tens+4 ones=1 hundred+12 tens+14 ones. Activity1 compare Lin and Jada’s representations of 582−145=437 and Jada's number-line 402−298=104 (298→300→400→402); source statement 'cannot decompose 402' is NOT correct: decompose a hundred then a ten. Activity2 535−214=321,700−589=111,683−398=285,918−608=310,735−457=278,602−487=115. Goal: Flexibly subtract with place-value decomposition OR counting on; critique false claim that zeros make decomposition impossible. Original media and open-task boundary: Lin/Jada source representations and open explanation are educator-judged; several legitimate methods per problem cannot be reduced to a single strategy label.","Exact mathematics and representations: Flexibly subtract with place-value decomposition OR counting on; critique false claim that zeros make decomposition impossible. CCSS source references: CCSS:2.NBT.A.1, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.9. Edulytics FIRST-PARTY example families are independent, not the original publisher question set.","Public student-facing authentic numbers and instructions: Warm-up equivalence of regrouped place-value statements; first source assertion 2 hundreds+3 tens+4 ones ≠2 hundreds+3 tens+14 ones, second =1 hundred+13 tens+4 ones, third 1 hundred+13 tens+4 ones=1 hundred+12 tens+14 ones. Activity1 compare Lin and Jada’s representations of 582−145=437 and Jada's number-line 402−298=104 (298→300→400→402); source statement 'cannot decompose 402' is NOT correct: decompose a hundred then a ten. Activity2 535−214=321,700−589=111,683−398=285,918−608=310,735−457=278,602−487=115. First-party companion questions generate fresh values, preserve the identified skill and separate paper/partner work from numeric self-check. Lin/Jada source representations and open explanation are educator-judged; several legitimate methods per problem cannot be reduced to a single strategy label.","Step1 identify source task and given quantities. Step2 represent with base-ten blocks, precise number line or appropriate chart; keep hidden publisher data unknown. Step3 identify units and whether compose/decompose none, a ten, a hundred, or both. Step4 calculate independent FIRST-PARTY numbers with explicit place-value regrouping or justified counting on. Step5 verify sum/subtraction using reverse operation and check number bounds. Step6 original card-sort/graph construction/partner game or flexible student explanation needs educator and never gets fabricated automatic certification. Source objective: Flexibly subtract with place-value decomposition OR counting on; critique false claim that zeros make decomposition impossible.","Avoid treating every operation as generic 3-digit arithmetic, neglecting a composed or decomposed ten/hundred, treating a personally selected method as uniquely correct or guessing unseen card/bar/image values. Source-specific safeguard: Lin/Jada source representations and open explanation are educator-judged; several legitimate methods per problem cannot be reduced to a single strategy label.","G2 U07 L16 Flexibly subtract with place-value decomposition OR counting on; critique false claim that zeros make decomposition impossible. Original evidence: Warm-up equivalence of regrouped place-value statements; first source assertion 2 hundreds+3 tens+4 ones ≠2 hundreds+3 tens+14 ones, second =1 hundred+13 tens+4 ones, third 1 hundred+13 tens+4 ones=1 hundred+12 tens+14 ones. Activity1 compare Lin and Jada’s representations of 582−145=437 and Jada's number-line 402−298=104 (298→300→400→402); source statement 'cannot decompose 402' is NOT correct: decompose a hundred then a ten. Activity2 535−214=321,700−589=111,683−398=285,918−608=310,735−457=278,602−487=115. Human limits: Lin/Jada source representations and open explanation are educator-judged; several legitimate methods per problem cannot be reduced to a single strategy label."}),
 new(119,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L17","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-17/preparation.html","394ce0a0fe5dba47575c368b508524e64350174ee97bfa2e5a17253978b99530",new string[]{"CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.8"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will practice adding and subtracting within 1,000.\nThis lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need. In Activity 1, you learn stage 6 of the Target Numbers center. In this new stage, called Add Hundreds, Tens, or Ones, you add hundreds, tens, and ones to get as close to 1,000 as possible.","Key ideas:\n- Add within 1,000\n- Subtract within 1,000\n- The addends are the quantities being combined and the sum is the resulting total; the expression must match the representation.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(34 - 9\\) \\(434 - 99\\) \\(367 - 98\\) \\(635 - 298\\)\nApproach: Represent the quantities or relationships clearly, apply the lesson idea, and check that the result answers the question.","Step 1: Identify what the task asks you to find or show.\nStep 2: Represent the important quantities or relationships.\nStep 3: Apply the key idea from the lesson.\nStep 4: Explain how the representation supports the result.\nStep 5: Check that the result matches the question.","Common mistake: jumping to an answer before checking that the representation matches the quantities and relationship in the task.","You will practice adding and subtracting within 1,000. This lesson is optional because it is an opportunity for extra practice that not all classes may need."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit7 Lesson17 Center Day 3 (optional). Source-specific authentic mathematical evidence: OPTIONAL Number Talk adjust subtrahend 34−9=25,434−99=335,367−98=269,635−298=337. Target Numbers Stage6: use THREE number cubes to add hundreds, tens, ones toward 1,000; Stage7: subtract hundreds, tens, ones toward zero; actual partner plays and prior Five in a Row and How Close centers may be chosen. Goal: Practice adding/subtracting hundreds/tens/ones through random and partner-selected original center activities, and use inverse operation to check. Original media and open-task boundary: Original random dice, choices, actual score closest to target, turn sequence and winners are not single-source numeric facts; teacher must observe.","Exact mathematics and representations: Practice adding/subtracting hundreds/tens/ones through random and partner-selected original center activities, and use inverse operation to check. CCSS source references: CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.8. Edulytics FIRST-PARTY example families are independent, not the original publisher question set.","Public student-facing authentic numbers and instructions: OPTIONAL Number Talk adjust subtrahend 34−9=25,434−99=335,367−98=269,635−298=337. Target Numbers Stage6: use THREE number cubes to add hundreds, tens, ones toward 1,000; Stage7: subtract hundreds, tens, ones toward zero; actual partner plays and prior Five in a Row and How Close centers may be chosen. First-party companion questions generate fresh values, preserve the identified skill and separate paper/partner work from numeric self-check. Original random dice, choices, actual score closest to target, turn sequence and winners are not single-source numeric facts; teacher must observe.","Step1 identify source task and given quantities. Step2 represent with base-ten blocks, precise number line or appropriate chart; keep hidden publisher data unknown. Step3 identify units and whether compose/decompose none, a ten, a hundred, or both. Step4 calculate independent FIRST-PARTY numbers with explicit place-value regrouping or justified counting on. Step5 verify sum/subtraction using reverse operation and check number bounds. Step6 original card-sort/graph construction/partner game or flexible student explanation needs educator and never gets fabricated automatic certification. Source objective: Practice adding/subtracting hundreds/tens/ones through random and partner-selected original center activities, and use inverse operation to check.","Avoid treating every operation as generic 3-digit arithmetic, neglecting a composed or decomposed ten/hundred, treating a personally selected method as uniquely correct or guessing unseen card/bar/image values. Source-specific safeguard: Original random dice, choices, actual score closest to target, turn sequence and winners are not single-source numeric facts; teacher must observe.","G2 U07 L17 Practice adding/subtracting hundreds/tens/ones through random and partner-selected original center activities, and use inverse operation to check. Original evidence: OPTIONAL Number Talk adjust subtrahend 34−9=25,434−99=335,367−98=269,635−298=337. Target Numbers Stage6: use THREE number cubes to add hundreds, tens, ones toward 1,000; Stage7: subtract hundreds, tens, ones toward zero; actual partner plays and prior Five in a Row and How Close centers may be chosen. Human limits: Original random dice, choices, actual score closest to target, turn sequence and winners are not single-source numeric facts; teacher must observe."}),
 new(120,"PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G2:U07:L18","https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-2/unit-7/lesson-18/preparation.html","56ca149685f55f25c2752ab02309f4c53232dd3970d62dcf6d63a19b104669ff",new string[]{"CCSS:2.MD.D.10","CCSS:2.NBT.B.7","CCSS:2.NBT.B.8","CCSS:2.NBT.B.9"},new string[]{"Learning goal: You will apply your understanding of bar graphs and addition and subtraction within 1,000 to write and solve questions that can be solved from information on a given bar graph.\nThis lesson is optional because it does not address any new mathematical content standards. This lesson does provide you with an opportunity to apply precursor skills of mathematical modeling. In this lesson, you analyze a bar graph that has some information obscured due to a paint splatter.","Key ideas:\n- Add or subtract 2 three-digit numbers using place value strategies that include composing or decomposing 2 units\n- Determine questions that can be answered by a given bar graph\n- Interpret data represented in a bar graph\n- Read category labels, values, and scales before comparing or calculating with a data display.","Example 1: Find the value of each expression mentally. \\(530-420\\) \\(530-426\\) \\(535-420\\) \\(535-426\\)\nExample 2: Oh no! Paint fell on the poster of the bar graph Clare and Priya created. Description: <span>Horizontal bar graph. Horizontal axis, Grades 1 to 4. Vertical axis partially covered with paint, part we can see is from 298 to 315 by ones. Grade 1, unclear. Grade 2, 298. Grade 3, 312. Grade 4, 305.</span> Answer the questions that you can with the graph. If a...","Step 1: Read the title, labels, categories, and scale.\nStep 2: Locate the values needed for the question.\nStep 3: Compare, add, subtract, or interpret the values as required.\nStep 4: State the result in the context of the data.","Common mistake: reading the wrong category or scale. Check the labels and scale before using the values.","You will apply your understanding of bar graphs and addition and subtraction within 1,000 to write and solve questions that can be solved from information on a given bar graph. This lesson is optional because it does not address any new mathematical content standards."},new string[]{"IM2021 G2 Unit7 Lesson18 Paint Splattered Bar Graph (optional). Source-specific authentic mathematical evidence: OPTIONAL original Paint Splattered Bar Graph: source bar chart partly obscured, learners must decide which original information and questions remain answerable, use addition/subtraction within 1,000, AUTHOR a new valid question from available data and trade questions with a classmate. Publisher source values obscured by paint must not be invented. Goal: Interpret graphical data under information limits: distinguish answerable questions from unknown bars, explain why, and compose/share original questions. Original media and open-task boundary: Actual original publisher bar figures and obscured values and student-authored questions NEED direct teacher review; invented Edulytics bar data may be marked only as NEW, never original.","Exact mathematics and representations: Interpret graphical data under information limits: distinguish answerable questions from unknown bars, explain why, and compose/share original questions. CCSS source references: CCSS:2.MD.D.10, CCSS:2.NBT.B.7, CCSS:2.NBT.B.8, CCSS:2.NBT.B.9. Edulytics FIRST-PARTY example families are independent, not the original publisher question set.","Public student-facing authentic numbers and instructions: OPTIONAL original Paint Splattered Bar Graph: source bar chart partly obscured, learners must decide which original information and questions remain answerable, use addition/subtraction within 1,000, AUTHOR a new valid question from available data and trade questions with a classmate. Publisher source values obscured by paint must not be invented. First-party companion questions generate fresh values, preserve the identified skill and separate paper/partner work from numeric self-check. Actual original publisher bar figures and obscured values and student-authored questions NEED direct teacher review; invented Edulytics bar data may be marked only as NEW, never original.","Step1 identify source task and given quantities. Step2 represent with base-ten blocks, precise number line or appropriate chart; keep hidden publisher data unknown. Step3 identify units and whether compose/decompose none, a ten, a hundred, or both. Step4 calculate independent FIRST-PARTY numbers with explicit place-value regrouping or justified counting on. Step5 verify sum/subtraction using reverse operation and check number bounds. Step6 original card-sort/graph construction/partner game or flexible student explanation needs educator and never gets fabricated automatic certification. Source objective: Interpret graphical data under information limits: distinguish answerable questions from unknown bars, explain why, and compose/share original questions.","Avoid treating every operation as generic 3-digit arithmetic, neglecting a composed or decomposed ten/hundred, treating a personally selected method as uniquely correct or guessing unseen card/bar/image values. Source-specific safeguard: Actual original publisher bar figures and obscured values and student-authored questions NEED direct teacher review; invented Edulytics bar data may be marked only as NEW, never original.","G2 U07 L18 Interpret graphical data under information limits: distinguish answerable questions from unknown bars, explain why, and compose/share original questions. Original evidence: OPTIONAL original Paint Splattered Bar Graph: source bar chart partly obscured, learners must decide which original information and questions remain answerable, use addition/subtraction within 1,000, AUTHOR a new valid question from available data and trade questions with a classmate. Publisher source values obscured by paint must not be invented. Human limits: Actual original publisher bar figures and obscured values and student-authored questions NEED direct teacher review; invented Edulytics bar data may be marked only as NEW, never original."})
